# Manufacturing Scheduling Convention

## Core Principle: Bottom-Up Scheduling

Dates flow **upward** from Work Orders to Manufacturing Orders, never the reverse.

## Rules

1. **WO defines duration**: Each `WorkOrderTask` has `estimated_duration_hours` (default 8h = 1 working day)
2. **MO defines start**: The user sets only `ManufacturingOrders.planned_start_at`
3. **MO end is calculated**: `planned_end_at` is computed from the WO dependency graph using `src/lib/scheduling.ts`
4. **Never set MO end manually**: The Schedule Tab calculates it from WO durations + dependencies

## Dependencies

- `WorkOrderTasks.depends_on_task_ids`: array of task IDs that must finish/start first
- `WorkOrderTasks.dependency_type`:
  - `finish_to_start` (default): cannot start until all dependencies complete
  - `start_to_start`: can start when dependencies start (parallel)
- Tasks with no dependencies start immediately at MO start

## Example

```
MO start: March 10
├── Cut Profiles (8h, no deps) → Mar 10 08:00 – Mar 10 17:00
├── Cut Fabric (4h, no deps)   → Mar 10 08:00 – Mar 10 12:00
├── Pick Hardware (4h, no deps) → Mar 10 08:00 – Mar 10 12:00
└── Assembly (16h, depends on all above, finish_to_start)
    → Mar 11 08:00 – Mar 12 17:00
MO end (calculated): March 12
```

## Release Check

An MO cannot be scheduled (`planned_start_at` set) unless:
- Work Orders are generated (tasks.length > 0)
- All WOs have estimated durations > 0
- All WOs have a Work Center assigned

## Working Hours

- 8 hours/day (08:00–17:00)
- Weekdays only (Mon–Fri)
- `src/lib/scheduling.ts` → `computeSchedule()` handles this

## Milestones

- `WorkOrderTasks.started_at` → actual start (set when status → in_progress)
- `WorkOrderTasks.completed_at` → actual end (set when status → completed)
- These are displayed as vertical markers on the Gantt in the Schedule Tab
